From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mt76: mt7921: do not remove a station the SoC firmware lost

A chip reset of the MT6897 during a connection gets the firmware running
again, but the connection doesn't come back. The next scan doesn't finish,
and the first command that waits for an answer times out and starts
another reset, at the latest the one from taking the interface down.

The reset work makes mac80211 disconnect, and that removes the station. The
power cycle of the reset took the station from the firmware already, and
told to remove a station it doesn't have, the firmware stops answering. A
query for its capabilities after each command of the disconnection shows
the place:

  command                     the query after it
  BSS activate (0x11)         answered
  EDCA (0x1d, 0xb0)           answered
  channel (0x1c), both        answered
  station removal (0x14)      no answer in 2 seconds

It is that command and not the state around it. It stops a firmware that
has had no connection since its start, and one that is connected, told to
remove another station. A query after each of the other commands of the
disconnection is answered, with the frames that go out meanwhile under the
old wcid. Why it stops is not known, no event comes after it.

The disconnection of the vendor driver doesn't name a station. A reset
there keeps the station records, and going idle deactivates the network,
which removes all stations of the BSS with another action of this command,
in cnmStaFreeAllStaByNetwork(). That action does no harm to a firmware
without stations, but has not been tried in a disconnection here.

Keep track of whether the firmware has the station, and leave the removal
out if not. The RX filter, lost with the firmware too, comes back without
help: mac80211 sets it again during the disconnection and the next
connection.

Seen on the device with a connection at 2.4 GHz and at 5 GHz, three resets
through debugfs on each, one of the three during HTTP requests: connected
again after 9.1 to 9.4 seconds, then DHCP and HTTP work. At 5 GHz, with a
second reset 3.4 to 7 seconds after the first, it took 9.2 to 18.2 seconds
from the second. At 2.4 GHz, a reset from a command that timed out during a
connection took 9.2 seconds. A disconnection without a reset still sends
the removal, and the firmware answers after it.
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc.c b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc.c
@@ -967,6 +967,9 @@
 	bool owned = powered;
 	int i, err;
 
+	/* The power cycle below takes it from the firmware */
+	mt7921_soc_of(dev)->fw_sta = false;
+
 	/*
 	 * With this set the NAPI leaves all replies to the wake work, which has
 	 * to wait for this work on the same queue. And here it stands for
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc.h b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc.h
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc.h
@@ -32,6 +32,8 @@
 	struct mtk_connsys *connsys;
 	/* The WF side and the WFDMA hang the bus if touched without this */
 	bool wf_on;
+	/* Whether the running firmware has been told about the access point */
+	bool fw_sta;
 	/* Where the ring registers point meanwhile */
 	struct mt76_queue_regs ring_scratch;
 	/* Rings short of window memory hear of nothing that would fill them */
diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc_mcu.c b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc_mcu.c
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc_mcu.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/mediatek/mt76/mt7921/soc_mcu.c
@@ -1157,6 +1157,7 @@
 {
 	struct mt792x_vif *mvif = (struct mt792x_vif *)vif->drv_priv;
 	enum nl80211_band band = mt7921_soc_vif_band(dev, mvif);
+	struct mt7921_soc *soc = mt7921_soc_of(dev);
 	struct ieee80211_sta_ht_cap *ht;
 	struct ieee80211_sta_vht_cap *vht;
 	struct ieee80211_sta_he_cap *he;
@@ -1182,9 +1183,20 @@
 		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
 
 	msta = (struct mt792x_sta *)sta->drv_priv;
-	if (!enable)
+	if (!enable) {
+		/*
+		 * After a chip reset mac80211 still has the station to remove.
+		 * The firmware stops answering when told to remove one it
+		 * doesn't have.
+		 */
+		if (!soc->fw_sta)
+			return 0;
+
+		soc->fw_sta = false;
+
 		return mt7921_soc_mcu_sta_remove(dev, mvif,
 						 &msta->deflink.wcid);
+	}
 
 	req = kzalloc_obj(*req, GFP_KERNEL);
 	if (!req)
@@ -1265,6 +1277,9 @@
 				sizeof(*req), assoc);
 	kfree(req);
 
+	if (!ret && !assoc)
+		soc->fw_sta = true;
+
 	return ret;
 }
 
